So all that makes the decision to move forward to new observability platform a lot more challenging.

28
00:03:17,680 --> 00:03:20,950
Right? So it is a challenging problem.

29
00:03:20,959 --> 00:03:23,509
But what is the primary driver?

30
00:03:23,509 --> 00:03:32,630
Do you have an example where a customer actually came to you to ground cover and said because of these reasons we wanted to move from our legacy observability tool set?

31
00:03:32,640 --> 00:03:43,750
Yeah, we recently had a customer with a thousand monitors which they didn't know how they built it.

32
00:03:43,750 --> 00:03:48,229
So the people that actually created those monitors are no longer in the organization.

33
00:03:48,229 --> 00:03:58,631
So imagine how frightening it is to now trying to migrate those monitors without knowing if what you're doing is actually the right thing. There is no way.

34
00:03:58,631 --> 00:04:07,110
So let's take a simple example of think about monitors that alert when specific log happens.

35
00:04:07,110 --> 00:04:09,750
It could be like a free text search.

36
00:04:09,750 --> 00:04:12,309
It could be specific attribute with specific value.

37
00:04:12,309 --> 00:04:15,990
Let's say status error.

38
00:04:15,990 --> 00:04:26,151
So now when you migrate those monitors, imagine that you have a mistake and basically you don't know that you are not covered by your monitors. So that's pretty scary.

39
00:04:26,151 --> 00:04:37,110
This specific customer, their requirement was to have a fully automated process that will make sure their monitors are being migrated successfully.

40
00:04:37,110 --> 00:04:42,230
And in order to do that, it actually it goes really deep.

41
00:04:42,230 --> 00:04:54,950
You need to understand the log structure and the transformation that each log is going through in order to ensure that those monitors are still covering you and basically that you can sleep good at night.

42
00:04:54,960 --> 00:04:57,030
Thousand monitors, that's quite a lot.

43
00:04:57,040 --> 00:04:58,950
Is that the norm?

44
00:04:58,950 --> 00:05:04,070
Do you have quite a few customers with that many dashboards and monitors?

45
00:05:04,070 --> 00:05:10,629
I think the average customer has few hundreds of monitors and at least dozens of dashboards.

46
00:05:10,629 --> 00:05:20,230
I think it really depends if the customers are using infrastructure as code to generate those monitors because it's usually very hard to create it manually.

47
00:05:20,240 --> 00:05:24,070
But yeah, we're seeing numbers of hundreds of monitors.

48
00:05:24,070 --> 00:05:26,870
We have customer with 5,000 monitors.

49
00:05:26,870 --> 00:05:29,830
That's the high bar. Like I think the biggest we have is 10,000 monitors.

50
00:05:29,840 --> 00:05:31,990
Wow.

51
00:05:31,990 --> 00:05:39,270
If you have taken all that effort to build thousand monitors and you know that migrating is going to be quite challenging, why go into that effort?

52
00:05:39,280 --> 00:05:44,230
What was the reason of saying that this does no longer works for me?

53
00:05:44,240 --> 00:05:50,469
Yeah, that's a good question and it comes back to the reason why we started ground cover.

54
00:05:50,469 --> 00:05:56,710
We were on the other side using some legacy vendor and basically you have two main challenges.

55
00:05:56,710 --> 00:06:03,189
One is that you need to generate the data in order to have all the data and you need to instrument your application.

56
00:06:03,189 --> 00:06:08,550
I'm talking about APM, open telemetry or whatever SDK you're using.

57
00:06:08,550 --> 00:06:12,950
That's really cool, but you don't know what you don't know.

58
00:06:12,950 --> 00:06:20,230
So if you didn't instrument your application, you basically don't have the information that actually what's going on.

59
00:06:20,230 --> 00:06:22,950
That's one reason you want more data.

60
00:06:22,950 --> 00:06:33,270
And the other reason that it's very very expensive old way of doing observability is basically send all the data to some SAS provider.

61
00:06:33,270 --> 00:06:38,390
But the reality is that it's very expensive not just even from licensing fee.

62
00:06:38,390 --> 00:06:48,390
Think about the egress fee that you need to pay in order to send all that data and that lead to some kind of a vicious cycle.

63
00:06:48,390 --> 00:06:57,670
So before that we said we want more data right and then we instrument our application we get more data and then the bill come and it's very very expensive.

64
00:06:57,670 --> 00:07:10,150
So now we are reducing the data that we just instrumented and that caused frustration and most organization have roles in the R&amp;D or devops that main concern is to make sure observability cost is under control.

65
00:07:10,160 --> 00:07:23,430
What we built in ground cover is the first bring your own cloud platform observability platform which means that the data plane is inside the customer account.

66
00:07:23,430 --> 00:07:29,029
So imagine that we provision a new AWS account or GCP or Azure whatever hyperscaler you're using.

67
00:07:29,029 --> 00:07:31,350
We deploy the back end.

68
00:07:31,350 --> 00:07:45,830
We have a control plane that manage it but all the data plane is inside the customer environment which means it's a lot more efficient and it allow us basically not to charge our customers by volume of data.

69
00:07:45,830 --> 00:07:52,469
So that also help in terms of the anxiety that most customer have around pricing.

70
00:07:52,469 --> 00:08:05,350
So that's one thing and the other thing that we did is we have our own ebpf sensor which generates a lot more data and because the customer doesn't pay by volume those two new techniques are

71
00:08:05,360 --> 00:08:18,309
actually amplifying each other in a very nice way right so if I understood it correctly the primary reason for firms aspiring to migrate from the existing observability tool set is

72
00:08:18,309 --> 00:08:28,070
that most of the legacy observability tool set relied on a lot of data and having that much data made it very expensive.

73
00:08:28,070 --> 00:08:34,070
Whereas new tool sets like ground cover are able to not send the data to your particular cloud.

74
00:08:34,070 --> 00:08:46,230
Whereas you can use your the firm's own cloud or use it on prem which reduces the cost and that's one of the primary drivers for firms aspiring to migrate out. Is that right?

75
00:08:46,240 --> 00:08:48,070
That's correct.

76
00:08:48,070 --> 00:08:58,949
And even more than that, so because we're not making money out of volume, that liberate the product team to basically be on the customer side, right?

77
00:08:58,949 --> 00:09:05,750
So imagine that you're trying to sell an observability platform and you make money out of data and you see the customer sending garbage data, right?

78
00:09:05,750 --> 00:09:10,230
We all know the phrase like garbage data in, garbage out.

79
00:09:10,230 --> 00:09:17,350
But from a seller perspective, if you're making money from that garbage data, you're in a conflict, right?

80
00:09:17,350 --> 00:09:27,190
Like we all want to believe that the vendor will encourage the customer to stop sending it but the reality is that they have zero incentive and actually hurt their business.

81
00:09:27,190 --> 00:09:35,350
So when we started ground cover we decided that we will always be on the customer side in terms of value.

82
00:09:35,350 --> 00:09:47,430
So our product team is constantly trying to reduce the infrastructure cost and making sure the customer have the right data that he needs.

83
00:09:47,430 --> 00:09:49,509
So think about like a simple example.

84
00:09:49,509 --> 00:09:54,870
You have an observability pipeline that drop logs by pattern by specific pattern that it's spammy.

85
00:09:54,870 --> 00:09:56,870
You have two options to do that, right?

86
00:09:56,870 --> 00:10:06,070
The old way, the legacy way is to make the customer send the data to the SAS platform and charge by ingest which could be very very expensive.

87
00:10:06,070 --> 00:10:12,150
In ground cover, we push down the filters to the sensor.

88
00:10:12,150 --> 00:10:20,630
So basically the sensor at the node level will not send those logs because we don't make money of it anyway.

89
00:10:20,630 --> 00:10:27,910
So why would we waste time and energy and money by serializing those logs, shipping it over network and then dropping it at the other edge?

90
00:10:27,910 --> 00:10:31,350
Because we're doing the same thing from both sides.

91
00:10:31,350 --> 00:10:37,829
We can just drop it at the node level and reduce a lot of cost and energy.

92
00:10:37,829 --> 00:10:46,389
So that's just one example of why this alignment matters, why the new model actually matters to all our customers.

93
00:10:46,389 --> 00:10:55,269
And what are some of the typical pain points you have seen during the migration process and if you can maybe bring it to life with some any examples that might come to mind?

94
00:10:55,279 --> 00:10:57,430
Yeah, sure.

95
00:10:57,430 --> 00:11:02,949
There's something that we called in the company observability for observability.

96
00:11:02,949 --> 00:11:05,829
So what it means is that the customer want to migrate, right?

97
00:11:05,829 --> 00:11:15,430
But they have 50 60 integrations active and they don't even know what integrations they have, right?

98
00:11:15,430 --> 00:11:28,230
because most of the integrations are configured somewhere else and they just send data and it's been done by dozens of people across the organization.

99
00:11:28,230 --> 00:11:35,430
So we had customer with I think it was 40 or 50 integrations with different cloud providers and different configurations

100
00:11:35,430 --> 00:11:43,750
and basically what we did in the migration process is we first start with discovery of what data you have.

101
00:11:43,750 --> 00:11:48,949
So it's looking at observability data from an observability perspective in a way.

102
00:11:48,949 --> 00:11:57,110
So we inspect the data, analyze it and say okay we found 50 active integrations according to the data

103
00:11:57,110 --> 00:12:08,150
that we're seeing and now we automatically translate each and every integration from each and every vendor to basically adapt to how it looks at ground cover integration.

104
00:12:08,150 --> 00:12:20,710
So the customer get like a report displaying those are the integrations that we found, those were the integrations that will be added and just click next and that will be done.

105
00:12:20,710 --> 00:12:28,949
So think about how much hard work you have to do just to get all those configurations from an existing integrations you have.

106
00:12:28,949 --> 00:12:34,230
This is almost impossible to do manually without mistakes.

107
00:12:34,230 --> 00:12:41,030
So that's like one simple aspect that actually starts the process of migration from most of our customers.

108
00:12:41,040 --> 00:12:43,110
Yep.

109
00:12:43,110 --> 00:12:45,110
Sounds very complicated and difficult task.

110
00:12:45,110 --> 00:12:49,509
Let's move to the next section where we discuss on approaches of migration.

111
00:12:49,509 --> 00:12:52,310
So what approaches have you seen firms take for migration?

112
00:12:52,310 --> 00:12:59,670
Is it you know phased approach or is it a big bang approach and are there other approaches that you have seen as well?

113
00:12:59,680 --> 00:13:11,350
That's a really good question and we actually we have our own opinion about what you should do first and how but we also very adaptive to what the customers want

114
00:13:11,350 --> 00:13:20,870
because sometimes the organizational needs are much more important than what we think are the right way but usually what happened is we first start with discovery.

115
00:13:20,870 --> 00:13:32,310
So we will fetch all your assets, dashboards, white, log pipelines, even policies in terms of user management and permissions and we first just visualize it.

116
00:13:32,310 --> 00:13:36,550
It's like getting you on boarded on what are we going to do here?

117
00:13:36,550 --> 00:13:40,069
Those are our tasks.

118
00:13:40,069 --> 00:13:44,710
From this point, you have a few options.

119
00:13:44,710 --> 00:13:47,829
We usually encourage to start by the most important assets.

120
00:13:47,829 --> 00:13:50,470
So how do we know what's important?

121
00:13:50,470 --> 00:13:53,670
we can get some metadata.

122
00:13:53,670 --> 00:14:05,430
So you can imagine that dashboards that are being edited in the recent week or month are probably more important than dashboard that haven't been touched for more than a year.

123
00:14:05,430 --> 00:14:19,030
So we get that information and also we can get information like how many views do you have for that dashboards and basically create some kind of a prioritization of the assets.

124
00:14:19,030 --> 00:14:27,509
So monitors that are firing right now or fired in the last week are probably critical for organization because they're actually happening, right?

125
00:14:27,509 --> 00:14:32,230
If there is a monitor that never been fired in the last year, it's probably less important.

126
00:14:32,230 --> 00:14:37,750
So that's one view that most of our customers find really really effective.

127
00:14:37,750 --> 00:14:44,470
But we have another view which is focus around what's missing.

128
00:14:44,470 --> 00:14:47,671
So let's say we miss some metrics. We got like 5,000 metrics.

129
00:14:47,680 --> 00:14:54,390
We mapped them and we fetched 4,900, right? So, we're missing 100 metrics.

130
00:14:54,399 --> 00:15:01,509
Now, how do you know which one is important?

131
00:15:01,509 --> 00:15:14,550
So, basically what we do is we fetch again all the assets that use those metrics or those signals and then we rank them by how popular they are, by the usage.

132
00:15:14,550 --> 00:15:19,910
So if there is one metric that is missing to accomplish 80% of the dashboard, let's focus on that.

133
00:15:19,910 --> 00:15:27,670
So basically those are the two angles that we're trying to attack that this hard process.

134
00:15:27,670 --> 00:15:30,390
And the main thing is to build trust, right? We we start this process.

135
00:15:30,399 --> 00:15:33,030
Some say it's like moving a bank.

136
00:15:33,030 --> 00:15:34,710
Some say it's like moving an apartment.

137
00:15:34,710 --> 00:15:36,629
I don't know which size I am.

138
00:15:36,629 --> 00:15:41,030
But the common thing is it's very stressful and there is a lot of fear around it.

139
00:15:41,030 --> 00:15:47,430
And our job is to give you the confidence we can do it.

140
00:15:47,430 --> 00:15:57,350
So we bring the tools to help you analyze what is going on, what is the progress, what's needed to achieve more progress.

141
00:15:57,350 --> 00:16:01,990
And the last part is the feedback loop that is very very important.

142
00:16:01,990 --> 00:16:07,829
So if you added that missing integration, you automatically visualize the data.

143
00:16:07,829 --> 00:16:22,310
you automatically see that those box checked and that's those kind of little games that help you make progress in this quest build the confidence that we can do it together and the last piece is bring all the all our R&amp;D like all the stakeholders

144
00:16:22,310 --> 00:16:30,949
and present them the process the results of what we did and let them play with it and give the feedback that's how we usually close a P

145
00:16:30,949 --> 00:16:38,630
or on boarding of a new customer and do you have any story or example where a particular migration did not go as per plan. What were the challenges?

146
00:16:38,639 --> 00:16:42,390
What went wrong? And how did you fix it?

147
00:16:42,399 --> 00:16:54,870
I think the most challenging part is getting all the observability pipelines right because usually when you work with customers,

148
00:16:54,870 --> 00:16:59,509
you see they have some kind of layers of software in their organization, right?

149
00:16:59,509 --> 00:17:08,949
We all have this service that we know we should fix but or we should change those log lines but no one is getting to it and it's pretty scary

150
00:17:08,949 --> 00:17:15,270
because it's an old monolith that 80% of the company rely on and nobody want to touch it.

151
00:17:15,270 --> 00:17:24,390
So around those software component built a lot of I would say legacy pipelines that we need to discover.

152
00:17:24,390 --> 00:17:33,029
So a good example was that we had one customer that had a few versions of the same service running together simultaneously.

153
00:17:33,029 --> 00:17:45,990
So they already started doing the work of migrating this service to a new logging solution but they roll it out gradually very very gradually.

154
00:17:45,990 --> 00:17:58,630
So our platform discovered those logs and we identified that basically the new logs as what is going on and we mapped those monitors and dashboards according to the data that we saw.

155
00:17:58,630 --> 00:18:07,110
What we missed is that at the same time we had the same service running on a older version with a different structure of logs.

156
00:18:07,110 --> 00:18:20,070
So we basically got tricked by different versions of that service and we missed some observability transformations that caused to some dashboards not to load or we were missing data in a way.

157
00:18:20,070 --> 00:18:24,390
The data was there but not in the structure and form that we expected it to be.

158
00:18:24,390 --> 00:18:33,190
The good thing is that this team was very very engaged and they knew exactly what they're looking for and they proved us show us this is the dashboard.

159
00:18:33,190 --> 00:18:43,430
we only see half of the data and we're like okay let's dive and see the actual raw data and once we did that we realized they had

160
00:18:43,430 --> 00:18:51,270
so many transformation happening in so many places but we learned from this experience and now our inspection on the data is

161
00:18:51,270 --> 00:18:58,870
so much better thanks to that specific case I really like those cases where we learn from our customers new things that we can leverage and share

162
00:18:58,880 --> 00:19:09,510
with our new customers basically that enjoy the hard work Yeah, it just brings out the complexity of the migration task and the challenges around it.

163
00:19:09,510 --> 00:19:14,870
How do you suggest migration workflows can be reframed to reduce fuel?

164
00:19:14,880 --> 00:19:19,510
That's a question we are dealing with a lot.

